<Window x:Class="EQBuddy.ProgressWindow"
        xmlns="http://schemas.microsoft.com/winfx/2006/xaml/presentation"
        xmlns:x="http://schemas.microsoft.com/winfx/2006/xaml"
        Title="EQBuddy Progress" SizeToContent="Height"
        WindowStyle="None" AllowsTransparency="True" Background="Transparent"
        Topmost="True" ShowInTaskbar="False" ResizeMode="CanResize"
        Width="520" WindowStartupLocation="Manual">
    <!-- The PROGRESS THEME (docs/Themes.md; David ruled themes a direction on
         2026-08-19: "part of the major UX revamp that organizes everything much
         better... just as we did with quests"). Five widget cards — Progress, Money,
         Motes, Faction, Raids — become four tabs here, and one launcher line on the
         widget.

         Why a window and not an overlay: every one of these is RETROSPECTIVE. Nothing
         on these tabs has a deadline attached, so by CLAUDE.md's surface rule none of
         it earns space over the running game. What it earns instead is room to be read.

         QuestsWindow is the template, and deliberately so — David: "I really want to
         use the approach for Quests as a guide for how we integrate." Same chrome, same
         EqSegmentedStrip tab row, same drag/close/zoom wiring. There is no LIST+DETAIL
         split here because there is nothing to select: each tab is one surface, already
         built, already lifted onto the IWidgetCard seam.

         Nothing below carries a literal size, radius or spacing value — every number
         resolves out of the token dictionary DesignSystem.Tokens() composes from
         EQBuddy.UI.Shared.DesignTokens. DesignRatchetTests fails the build if a literal
         reappears. -->
    <Border Background="{DynamicResource BgBrush}" CornerRadius="{DynamicResource CornerPanel}"
            BorderBrush="{DynamicResource HairlineBrush}" BorderThickness="1"
            MouseLeftButtonDown="OnDrag">
      <Grid>
        <Grid.RowDefinitions>
          <RowDefinition Height="Auto"/>
          <RowDefinition Height="Auto"/>
          <RowDefinition Height="*"/>
          <RowDefinition Height="Auto"/>
        </Grid.RowDefinitions>

        <!-- Title row. The icon is a vector (DesignSystem.Icon), never a glyph — emoji
             box outright in the Wine prefixes the Linux and macOS builds run under
             (#148, #166). -->
        <Grid Grid.Row="0" Margin="{DynamicResource PadCard}">
            <StackPanel x:Name="TitleRow" Orientation="Horizontal" VerticalAlignment="Center"/>
            <Button x:Name="CloseBtn" Style="{StaticResource EqIconButton}" ToolTip="Close"
                    HorizontalAlignment="Right" Click="OnClose"/>
        </Grid>

        <StackPanel Grid.Row="1" Margin="{DynamicResource PadCard}">
            <!-- Labels, order and keys come from Core's ProgressSurface, and the badges
                 beside them from UI.Shared's ProgressTheme, so this window, the Avalonia
                 widget and EQBuddy Mobile cannot end up naming four different tabs or
                 reporting four different numbers (#184, #210). -->
            <!-- WRAPS, and it has to. A horizontal StackPanel measures its children with
                 INFINITE width, so nothing ever reaches a boundary to wrap at — the
                 fourth chip is simply clipped at the panel's edge, silently, with no
                 ellipsis to say so. That is trap 14 with chips instead of text, and it is
                 the same bug #184 hit when the class strip clipped at NEC. Found here by
                 looking at the first screenshot: the strip showed Experience, Wealth and
                 Faction, and Raids was not on screen at all. These badges carry real
                 sentences ("16.0% xp, +1 lvl (2 new), +1 aa"), so four of them will not
                 fit one 520px row and were never going to. -->
            <WrapPanel x:Name="TabStrip" Margin="{DynamicResource StackS}"/>
        </StackPanel>

        <ScrollViewer Grid.Row="2" x:Name="BodyScroll" VerticalScrollBarVisibility="Auto"
                      Margin="{DynamicResource PadCard}">
            <!-- The active tab's surface. It carries no Visibility and no Margin of its
                 own: the lifted control owns both (trap 15 — a control that hides itself
                 inside a host that also hides itself has two switches for one state, and
                 only one of them is ever wired). -->
            <ContentControl x:Name="TabBody"/>
        </ScrollViewer>

        <!-- The three mini-dashboard stars that used to sit on the Progress, Money and
             Motes card headers. They come here rather than onto the launcher card for
             two reasons: three identical stars in one card header say nothing about
             which is which, and nesting a ToggleButton inside a Button makes its Click
             bubble to the Button's own handler, so every star press would also open this
             window. Here they get the words they never had on the widget.

             They had to go SOMEWHERE. The star is the only writer MiniStats has for
             these three keys, and a fold that drops the last writer of a setting is
             exactly the shape of #204/#209, #210 and #212 (CLAUDE.md trap 20). -->
        <StackPanel Grid.Row="3" x:Name="MiniRow" Orientation="Horizontal"
                    Margin="{DynamicResource PadCard}" VerticalAlignment="Center"/>
      </Grid>
    </Border>
</Window>
